            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>How 800K RMB Was Wasted in One Campaign</h2>
<p>A utility app team decided to expand into Indonesia and Brazil in Q2 2023, with a monthly budget of 800K RMB and an expected 150K new users. After two weeks of operation, they discovered: Indonesia's CPM reached $12 but conversion rate was below 0.8%, while Brazil's ad accounts were frequently suspended. The team urgently paused the campaign in the third week.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Problem Analysis</strong>: This wasn't a channel issue or budget problem. The root cause was the crude logic of simply machine-translating Chinese content to English and launching it in emerging markets.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 1: Assuming English Works Everywhere</h2>
<p><strong>Phenomenon</strong>: Many teams believe Southeast Asian and Latin American users have sufficient English proficiency, directly using English creatives.</p>
<p><strong>Decision Criteria</strong>:<br />
- Indonesia's English penetration is about 30%, mainly in first-tier cities<br />
- Brazilian Portuguese speakers account for 99%; English content sees 40% lower CTR<br />
- Philippines has high English penetration, but colloquial expressions differ significantly</p>
<p><strong>Action Steps</strong>:<br />
1. Indonesia must have Indonesian subtitles or voiceovers<br />
2. Brazil must use local Portuguese, avoiding Portuguese-English direct translation<br />
3. Philippines can use English, but must adapt to local slang and expressions</p>
<p><strong>Result</strong>: After adjusting language strategy, the team's Indonesia CPA decreased by 35%, Brazil CPA decreased by 28%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 2: Using Domestic Viral Creatives Directly Overseas</h2>
<p><strong>Phenomenon</strong>: Domestic viral content featuring pretty girls dancing or celebrity endorsements, directly uploaded to TikTok and Meta for Southeast Asia and Latin America.</p>
<p><strong>Decision Criteria</strong>:<br />
- Target market users are naturally wary of "over-performed" ads<br />
- Local users trust real user sharing and scene demonstrations more<br />
- In some cultures, overly entertaining ads are perceived as "unreliable"</p>
<p><strong>Action Steps</strong>:<br />
- Indonesia: Increase real-user testing and tutorial content, adapt to local KOL styles<br />
- Brazil: Emphasize family scenarios and social sharing elements; Brazilians love content "to show friends"<br />
- Overall Latin America: Avoid overly polished packaging, use "down-to-earth" filming styles</p>
<p><strong>Result</strong>: After changing creative styles, CTR increased by 1.5x, and user retention improved by 20%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 3: Ignoring Payment Methods and Channel Differences</h2>
<p><strong>Phenomenon</strong>: Only integrating Google Play and App Store payments, ignoring local payment solutions.</p>
<p><strong>Decision Criteria</strong>:<br />
- Indonesia: GoPay, OVO, DANA account for over 60%; credit card penetration below 15%<br />
- Brazil: Boleto bank transfer and Pix e-payment are mainstream; credit card installment needs are strong<br />
- Vietnam: MoMo wallet has high usage; carrier billing is an important supplement</p>
<p><strong>Action Steps</strong>:<br />
1. Prioritize displaying local mainstream payment methods on in-app or landing pages<br />
2. Localize payment button copy (not simple translation, but according to local expression habits)<br />
3. Configure local customer service channels (WhatsApp is core in Latin America)</p>
<p><strong>Result</strong>: After optimizing the payment flow, the app's paid conversion rate increased from 1.2% to 2.8%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 4: Copying Domestic Account Structure and Strategy</h2>
<p><strong>Phenomenon</strong>: One ad account targeting 5 countries, budget allocation entirely based on "feeling."</p>
<p><strong>Decision Criteria</strong>:<br />
- Southeast Asia's six countries (Indonesia, Vietnam, Thailand, Philippines, Malaysia, Singapore) have significantly different user behaviors<br />
- Latin America requires separate account management for Brazil, Mexico, and Argentina<br />
- Different countries have different time zones; ad scheduling needs individual settings</p>
<p><strong>Action Steps</strong>:<br />
- Split ad accounts by language + region (separate account for Indonesia, separate for Portuguese-speaking Latin America)<br />
- Test at least 3 different ad variables for each market<br />
- Configure local timezone-based ad operations personnel or use automation rules</p>
<p><strong>Result</strong>: After splitting account operations, overall ROAS improved by 42%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>The Essence of Localization: Not Translation, But "Redesign"</h2>
<p>Returning to the opening case: if the team had conducted a systematic localization audit before launching, they could have avoided at least 60% of budget waste. Localization isn't converting Chinese to foreign languages—it's a comprehensive reconstruction from user psychology, payment habits, content aesthetics to advertising strategy.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended Action Checklist</strong>:<br />
- Pre-launch: Complete user profiling and competitive analysis for target markets<br />
- Creative preparation: Prepare at least 3 sets of different localized versions<br />
- Channel configuration: Confirm local payment methods and customer service channels<br />
- Account architecture: Split by market, avoid "one-pot cooking"<br />
- Continuous optimization: Establish localized A/B testing mechanism</p>
